WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.947
A z-score tells us how far a value is from the mean in units of standard deviation.

00:00:05.947 --> 00:00:11.492
The formula is the value minus the mean, divided by the standard deviation.

00:00:11.492 --> 00:00:17.462
In this example, we calculate the mean and standard deviation of five observations,

00:00:17.462 --> 00:00:21.112
then apply the formula to the entire array at once.

00:00:21.112 --> 00:00:24.017
Click Run and inspect the z-scores.

00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:26.937
Values near zero are close to the mean.

00:00:26.937 --> 00:00:29.692
Positive z-scores are above the mean, and negative z-scores are below it.

00:00:29.692 --> 00:00:37.307
Standardization is useful when variables are measured on different scales.

00:00:37.307 --> 00:00:41.122
For example, one feature might be measured in dollars while another is measured in years.

00:00:41.122 --> 00:00:49.142
In production machine-learning workflows, we often use a fitted scaler from scikit-learn rather than manually

00:00:49.142 --> 00:00:51.942
applying this formula.

00:00:51.942 --> 00:00:57.578
But understanding the NumPy calculation helps you understand what the scaler is doing conceptually.
